    <ul>
      <li><strong>Financial Crimes:</strong> Online banking fraud, phishing emails that trick you into revealing passwords, credit card theft, cryptocurrency scams.</li>
      <li><strong>Data Crimes:</strong> Stealing personal or corporate data, ransomware attacks that encrypt files and demand money, data leaks.</li>
      <li><strong>Crimes Against Persons:</strong> Cyberstalking, cyberbullying, revenge pornography, identity theft, defamation online.</li>
      <li><strong>Infrastructure Attacks:</strong> Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks on hospitals, power grids, or banking systems.</li>
      <li><strong>State-Sponsored Crimes:</strong> Government-backed hacking groups targeting another nation's critical infrastructure or elections.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">📊 Scale of the Problem</div>
      <p>According to global cybersecurity reports, cybercrime costs the world over <strong>₹600 lakh crore (approx. $8 trillion USD)</strong> annually. India alone witnessed a surge of over <strong>50,000+ registered cybercrime cases</strong> in recent years. This is why a robust legal framework is not optional — it is essential.</p>
