    <h2>2. What are Cyber Laws?</h2>

    <p><strong>Cyber laws</strong> (also called <em>Internet laws</em> or <em>IT laws</em>) are the body of legal rules and regulations that govern activities conducted over computers, digital networks, and the internet. They define what is legal and what is illegal in the digital world, just as traffic laws define rules for the road.</p>

    <div class="analogy">
      <div class="analogy-label">Analogy</div>
      <p>Think of the internet as a massive city. In a physical city, you have traffic rules, property laws, contract laws, and criminal laws to maintain order. Cyber laws are the equivalent of those rules — but for the digital city. Without them, anyone could steal, cheat, trespass, or destroy without consequence.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What Do Cyber Laws Cover?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Electronic Commerce (e-Commerce):</strong> Legal validity of online contracts, digital signatures, and electronic transactions.</li>
      <li><strong>Data Protection &amp; Privacy:</strong> Rules about collecting, storing, and sharing personal data.</li>
      <li><strong>Intellectual Property:</strong> Copyright and trademark protection for digital content, software, and creative works.</li>
      <li><strong>Cybercrime &amp; Penalties:</strong> Definitions of offences like hacking, identity theft, and spreading malware, along with punishments.</li>
      <li><strong>Electronic Evidence:</strong> Rules for how digital evidence (emails, screenshots, logs) is admissible in court.</li>
      <li><strong>Jurisdiction:</strong> Which country's law applies when a crime crosses borders.</li>
      <li><strong>Freedom of Speech Online:</strong> Balancing freedom of expression with restrictions on harmful content.</li>
    </ul>

