  <section id="s5">
    <div class="section-tag">Deep Dive</div>
    <h2>5. Key Sections of the IT Act 2000</h2>

    <p>The IT Act contains 94 Sections and 4 Schedules. Below are the most critical sections that investigators, lawyers, and citizens must understand:</p>

    <h3>Section 43 — Penalty for Damage to Computer Systems</h3>
    <p>This is a <em>civil liability</em> section. If anyone without authorisation accesses a computer, downloads data, introduces viruses, or causes disruption, they can be held liable to pay <strong>compensation up to ₹1 crore</strong> to the affected party. No criminal prosecution is needed — the victim can directly claim compensation.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">Example — Section 43</div>
      <p>A competitor company sends a virus to a company's server that corrupts a year's worth of financial records. Under Section 43, the victim company can claim full compensation for recovery costs, lost business, and damages — without needing to prove criminal intent.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Section 65 — Tampering with Computer Source Documents</h3>
    <p>Anyone who knowingly conceals, destroys, alters, or causes another person to alter computer source code (required to be kept or maintained by law) shall be punished with <strong>imprisonment up to 3 years</strong> or a fine of up to <strong>₹2 lakh</strong>, or both. This section protects software integrity and is commonly invoked in cases involving software piracy and academic fraud.</p>

    <h3>Section 66 — Computer-Related Offences (Hacking)</h3>
    <p>This is the primary <strong>hacking provision</strong>. If any act referred to in Section 43 (unauthorised access, downloading, introducing virus, causing disruption) is done <em>dishonestly or fraudulently</em>, it becomes a criminal offence punishable by:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Imprisonment up to <strong>3 years</strong>, OR</li>
      <li>Fine up to <strong>₹5 lakh</strong>, OR</li>
      <li>Both</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Section 66A — Online Offensive Content (Struck Down)</h3>
    <p>Originally criminalised sending "offensive" or "menacing" online messages. This section was <strong>unanimously struck down by the Supreme Court of India</strong> in <em>Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015)</em> as unconstitutional for violating Article 19(1)(a) — the fundamental right to freedom of speech and expression. It was too vague and was being misused to suppress legitimate dissent.</p>

    <div class="callout danger">
      <div class="callout-title">🚫 Section 66A — Struck Down by Supreme Court</div>
      <p>The Supreme Court held in 2015 that Section 66A was overbroad and vague, giving authorities unbridled power to arrest anyone for any online post that someone found "offensive." The ruling was a landmark victory for digital free speech in India.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Section 66B — Receiving Stolen Computer Resources</h3>
    <p>Dishonestly receiving or retaining any stolen computer resource or communication device is punishable by imprisonment up to <strong>3 years</strong> or fine up to <strong>₹1 lakh</strong>, or both. It is the cyber equivalent of "receiving stolen goods."</p>

    <h3>Section 66C — Identity Theft</h3>
    <p>Fraudulently or dishonestly using another person's electronic signature, password, or unique identification feature: imprisonment up to <strong>3 years</strong> and fine up to <strong>₹1 lakh</strong>. This covers stealing someone's Aadhaar OTP, passwords, or digital signature.</p>

    <h3>Section 66D — Cheating by Impersonation Using Computer Resources</h3>
    <p>Cheating someone by impersonating another person using a computer resource or communication device: imprisonment up to <strong>3 years</strong> and fine up to <strong>₹1 lakh</strong>. Classic examples include creating fake social media profiles to deceive others.</p>

    <h3>Section 66E — Violation of Privacy</h3>
    <p>Intentionally capturing, publishing, or transmitting someone's private area image without their consent: imprisonment up to <strong>3 years</strong> or fine up to <strong>₹2 lakh</strong>, or both. This directly addresses cases of hidden camera abuse, voyeurism, and revenge pornography.</p>

    <h3>Section 66F — Cyber Terrorism</h3>
    <p>The most serious provision. Committing or conspiring to commit acts that threaten national security, unity, sovereignty, or cause terror by targeting computers or networks — includes acts that could result in death, damage to property, or disruption of essential services. Punishment: <strong>imprisonment for life</strong>.</p>

    <h3>Section 67 — Publishing Obscene Material Online</h3>
    <p>Publishing or transmitting obscene material in electronic form: on first conviction, imprisonment up to <strong>3 years</strong> and fine up to <strong>₹5 lakh</strong>; on subsequent conviction, up to <strong>5 years</strong> and fine up to <strong>₹10 lakh</strong>.</p>

    <h3>Section 69 — Power to Monitor and Decrypt Information</h3>
    <p>The government can direct any agency to intercept, monitor, or decrypt information passing through any computer resource if it is necessary in the interest of sovereignty, security, friendly relations with foreign states, or public order. This is India's primary legal basis for lawful surveillance.</p>

    <h3>Section 72 — Breach of Confidentiality and Privacy</h3>
    <p>If any person who has obtained information from a computer system in exercise of their powers under the IT Act discloses it to a third party without authorisation: imprisonment up to <strong>2 years</strong> or fine up to <strong>₹1 lakh</strong>, or both.</p>

    <h3>Section 79 — Safe Harbour for Intermediaries</h3>
    <p>Network service providers (like internet service providers, social media platforms) are <em>not liable</em> for third-party information or data made available by them if they had no knowledge of the unlawful act, and upon receiving knowledge or notice, they expeditiously remove or disable access to that material. This "safe harbour" provision is crucial for platforms like YouTube, Facebook, and Twitter.</p>

