  <!-- ═══ SECTION 7 ═══ -->
  <section id="s7">
    <div class="section-tag">Evidence Law</div>
    <h2>7. Section 65B — Indian Evidence Act (IEA)</h2>

    <p>If IT Act 2000 is the "crime law" of the digital world, then <strong>Section 65B of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872</strong> is its courtroom companion — the rule that decides whether digital evidence can even be shown to a judge. This section is arguably the most practically important provision for any cyber investigator, forensic analyst, or criminal lawyer in India.</p>

    <h3>The Problem Section 65B Solves</h3>

    <div class="analogy">
      <div class="analogy-label">Analogy</div>
      <p>Imagine a murder case where the key witness is a photograph. But the photograph is a print of a digital image stored in a camera. Can you just show the printed photo to the court? What stops someone from claiming the photo was photoshopped or the print was tampered with? Section 65B creates a formal, certified bridge between the original digital data and its physical reproduction, ensuring courts can trust what they see.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Section 65B was inserted into the Indian Evidence Act by the IT Act 2000. It governs the <strong>admissibility of electronic records as evidence</strong> in courts. Before 65B, courts had no clear standard for accepting printed emails, screenshots, CDRs (Call Detail Records), or computer-generated reports as evidence.</p>

    <h3>What is an "Electronic Record"?</h3>
    <p>Under the IT Act 2000, an <strong>electronic record</strong> means "data, record or data generated, image or sound stored, received or sent in an electronic form or micro film or computer generated micro fiche." This includes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Emails and email attachments</li>
      <li>WhatsApp chats, SMS messages</li>
      <li>CCTV footage</li>
      <li>Call Detail Records (CDRs) and tower dump data</li>
      <li>Internet browsing history logs</li>
      <li>Computer-generated reports and outputs</li>
      <li>Bank transaction logs</li>
      <li>Social media posts and screenshots</li>
      <li>Audio and video recordings stored digitally</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The Four Conditions of Section 65B(2)</h3>
    <p>For any electronic record to be admissible in court, the computer that produced it must satisfy <strong>all four</strong> of the following conditions:</p>

