    <h3>Section 65B(4) — The Certificate Requirement</h3>
    <p>Along with the electronic record, a <strong>certificate</strong> must be produced. This certificate is issued by a <em>responsible official</em> (someone in charge of the computer or someone occupying a responsible position in relation to the operation of the relevant device). The certificate must:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Identify the electronic record containing the statement</li>
      <li>Describe the manner in which the electronic record was produced</li>
      <li>Give particulars of the device involved in the production (computer specifications, storage type)</li>
      <li>Deal with any of the conditions mentioned in Section 65B(2)</li>
      <li>Be <strong>signed</strong> by a person occupying a responsible official position</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠️ Critical Point for Investigators</div>
      <p>Without a properly executed Section 65B certificate, electronic evidence — no matter how damning — may be ruled inadmissible by the court. This is a fatal flaw in many cybercrime prosecutions in India. The certificate must be obtained <em>before</em> or <em>at the time of</em> submitting electronic records to court.</p>
    </div>
