  <section id="s8">
    <div class="section-tag">Procedure</div>
    <h2>8. How to Certify Electronic Evidence — Step-by-Step</h2>

    <p>Suppose a cybercrime investigator wants to produce a suspect's WhatsApp chat log as evidence in court. Here is the exact procedure to follow:</p>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Identify the Source Computer/Device</strong>
          <p>Determine which computer, server, or device stores the original electronic record. For WhatsApp chats, the relevant device is typically the phone or the WhatsApp server. Document the device's make, model, operating system, and storage specifications.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Create a Forensic Image (Hash-Verified Copy)</strong>
          <p>Use a write-blocker and forensic imaging tools (like FTK Imager or dd) to create a bit-for-bit forensic copy of the storage. Compute the <strong>MD5 and SHA-256 hash values</strong> of both the original and the copy — these hashes must match to prove the copy is identical to the original (no tampering).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Extract the Relevant Records</strong>
          <p>From the forensic copy, extract only the relevant electronic records (e.g., chat logs, emails, screenshots). Document the extraction process, tools used, and hash values of extracted files.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Prepare the Section 65B Certificate</strong>
          <p>Draft the certificate in a prescribed format (the law does not mandate a rigid format, but it must include all four conditions of 65B(2)). The certificate must be signed by the <em>responsible official</em> who managed the computer system.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Attach Certificate to the Electronic Record Output</strong>
          <p>The printout or electronic copy of the record must be physically attached to — or filed alongside — the 65B certificate when submitted to the court as evidence. Both are submitted together.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>File Before Court / Investigating Officer</strong>
          <p>The entire package (printout + certificate) is filed with the Court or Magistrate as a document. The certificate is treated as a legal document; making a false declaration in it is a criminal offence under the IT Act.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Who Can Issue a Section 65B Certificate?</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Source of Evidence</th><th>Appropriate Certifying Person</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Company's email server</td><td>IT Manager or CTO of the company</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bank transaction logs</td><td>Designated IT Officer of the bank</td></tr>
        <tr><td>WhatsApp / social media (from device)</td><td>Forensic examiner / investigating officer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CCTV footage (from system)</td><td>Person in charge of the CCTV system</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ISP server logs</td><td>Nodal Officer of the ISP</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hospital records (electronic)</td><td>Medical records officer of the hospital</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

