  <!-- ═══ SECTION 9 ═══ -->
  <section id="s9">
    <div class="section-tag">Case Studies</div>
    <h2>9. Landmark Case Studies</h2>

    <h3>Case 1 — Anvar P.V. v. P.K. Basheer (2014) — Supreme Court</h3>
    <div class="callout case">
      <div class="callout-title">📋 Case Summary</div>
      <p><strong>Facts:</strong> This was an election petition case where the petitioner wanted to submit a CD containing a speech as evidence. The CD was submitted to the court without a Section 65B certificate.<br><br>
      <strong>Held:</strong> The Supreme Court ruled that a Section 65B certificate is <em>mandatory</em> for admitting any electronic record. Without it, even secondary evidence under Sections 63 and 65 of the Indian Evidence Act cannot be used as a substitute for electronic records. The CD without the certificate was inadmissible.<br><br>
      <strong>Significance:</strong> This ruling fundamentally changed how electronic evidence is handled in Indian courts. It made the 65B certificate a non-negotiable prerequisite — not just a formality.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Case 2 — Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015) — Supreme Court</h3>
    <div class="callout case">
      <div class="callout-title">📋 Case Summary</div>
      <p><strong>Facts:</strong> Two young women in Mumbai were arrested under Section 66A of the IT Act for posting Facebook comments critical of a political leader's death and the resulting shutdown of Mumbai. This triggered a massive legal challenge.<br><br>
      <strong>Held:</strong> A 5-judge Constitutional Bench of the Supreme Court unanimously struck down Section 66A as unconstitutional. The court held that the section was overbroad and vague — the terms "grossly offensive," "menacing character," and "causing annoyance" had no clear legal boundaries.<br><br>
      <strong>Significance:</strong> A landmark ruling for freedom of speech on the internet in India. The court distinguished between <em>discussion, advocacy, and incitement</em> — only incitement to violence can be restricted; mere offensive speech cannot be.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Case 3 — State (NCT of Delhi) v. Navjot Sandhu (2005) — Parliament Attack Case</h3>
    <div class="callout case">
      <div class="callout-title">📋 Case Summary</div>
      <p><strong>Facts:</strong> Mobile phone CDRs (Call Detail Records) were used as primary evidence in the 2001 Parliament attack case to prove the conspiracy. The defence challenged their admissibility since no 65B certificate had been produced.<br><br>
      <strong>Held:</strong> The Supreme Court held that while the CDRs should have been accompanied by a 65B certificate, secondary oral evidence from the telecom company's officials could substitute in that specific case. However, this was a pre-<em>Anvar P.V.</em> ruling and was subsequently overruled in its liberal interpretation.<br><br>
      <strong>Significance:</strong> Highlighted the critical role of CDRs in criminal investigations and the importance of following proper evidence procedures.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Case 4 — Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal (2020) — Supreme Court</h3>
    <div class="callout case">
      <div class="callout-title">📋 Case Summary</div>
      <p><strong>Facts:</strong> This case clarified and reaffirmed the <em>Anvar P.V.</em> ruling. The court examined whether Section 65B certificate could be given after the electronic record is produced, or whether it must accompany the record from the start.<br><br>
      <strong>Held:</strong> The Supreme Court held that the Section 65B certificate is a <em>condition precedent</em> to admissibility — it must be produced when the document is first tendered in evidence. However, courts can exercise discretion to allow the certificate to be produced at a later stage in genuinely difficult circumstances, but this is not a blanket licence for delay.<br><br>
      <strong>Significance:</strong> Provided detailed procedural guidance on 65B certificates and confirmed the mandatory nature of the certificate, while providing some procedural flexibility.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

