  <!-- ═══ SECTION 10 ═══ -->
  <section id="s10">
    <div class="section-tag">Analysis</div>
    <h2>10. Advantages &amp; Disadvantages of Cyber Laws</h2>

    <h3>Advantages</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Advantage</th><th>Details</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Legal Recognition of Digital World</td><td>Gives electronic contracts, signatures, and records the same legal standing as physical documents, enabling a trillion-dollar digital economy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Deterrence Against Cybercrime</td><td>Clearly defined offences and substantial penalties (including life imprisonment for cyber terrorism) deter potential criminals.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumer Protection</td><td>Data protection provisions under Section 43A hold companies accountable for securing customers' personal data.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Facilitates e-Governance</td><td>Enables government services to be delivered digitally — tax filing, Aadhaar authentication, digital court filings — creating efficiency and reducing corruption.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Standardises Evidence</td><td>Section 65B provides a clear framework for using digital evidence in court, giving law enforcement and the judiciary common ground.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>International Alignment</td><td>Brings India in line with global cybersecurity norms, facilitating mutual legal assistance treaties (MLATs) and international cybercrime cooperation.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Disadvantages &amp; Criticisms</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Disadvantage</th><th>Details</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Outdated Rapidly</td><td>Technology changes faster than legislation. The 2000 Act couldn't anticipate smartphones, social media, AI, or cloud computing. Amendments lag behind reality.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vague Provisions</td><td>Struck-down Section 66A showed how vague language can be weaponised. Critics argue that Section 69 (surveillance powers) is also too broad.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Poor Enforcement Infrastructure</td><td>Many police officers lack the technical expertise to investigate cybercrimes effectively. Forensic labs are understaffed and under-equipped.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Low Conviction Rates</td><td>Despite rising cybercrime complaints, conviction rates remain low — partly due to evidence handling failures (like missing 65B certificates).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inadequate Privacy Law</td><td>Until the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023, India lacked a comprehensive privacy law. The IT Act's data protection provisions were insufficient for the scale of India's digital economy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jurisdictional Challenges</td><td>Cybercriminals operating from foreign jurisdictions (especially countries without extradition treaties) are almost impossible to bring to justice under Indian law alone.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Under-reporting of Crimes</td><td>Victims often don't report cybercrimes due to stigma (especially in cases involving privacy violations), lack of awareness, or fear that nothing will be done.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

