  <section id="s12">
    <div class="section-tag">Enforcement</div>
    <h2>12. Cyber Law Enforcement in India</h2>

    <h3>Key Bodies &amp; Agencies</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>CERT-In (Computer Emergency Response Team – India):</strong> The national nodal agency for cybersecurity incident response, under the Ministry of Electronics and IT (MeitY). Coordinates responses to major cyber incidents.</li>
      <li><strong>CBI (Central Bureau of Investigation):</strong> Handles major cybercrime cases, especially those with interstate or international dimensions.</li>
      <li><strong>State Cyber Cells:</strong> Each state has dedicated cybercrime police stations and cells (e.g., Delhi Cyber Crime Unit, Maharashtra Cyber, Telangana Cyber Security Bureau).</li>
      <li><strong>National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal (cybercrime.gov.in):</strong> A centralised portal for citizens to report cybercrime online. Reports are routed to relevant police jurisdictions.</li>
      <li><strong>Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C):</strong> A Ministry of Home Affairs initiative to combat cybercrime in a coordinated manner. Includes the National Cybercrime Threat Analytics Unit (NCTAU).</li>
      <li><strong>Adjudicating Officers:</strong> Officers appointed under the IT Act to adjudicate claims for compensation under Sections 43, 43A, and 72 — these are civil proceedings, not criminal.</li>
      <li><strong>Cyber Appellate Tribunal (CAT):</strong> The appellate body for decisions of Adjudicating Officers under the IT Act.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to File a Cybercrime Complaint in India</h3>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Preserve Evidence</strong>
          <p>Take screenshots, save emails, note URLs, and preserve any logs before reporting. Do not delete or alter anything. This raw evidence is critical.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>File on cybercrime.gov.in</strong>
          <p>Visit the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal and file a complaint. For financial fraud, use the helpline <strong>1930</strong> (the national cybercrime helpline) for immediate assistance — reports filed within the golden hour increase recovery chances.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Visit the Local Cyber Cell</strong>
          <p>Visit your nearest cyber crime police station with printed copies of evidence and a written complaint. Get an acknowledgment receipt.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num"></div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>FIR and Investigation</strong>
          <p>If a cognisable offence is established, police will register an FIR. Cyber forensic teams examine devices and logs. 65B certificates are collected during investigation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

