  <!-- ═══ SECTION 13 ═══ -->
  <section id="s13">
    <div class="section-tag">Global Context</div>
    <h2>13. Global Cyber Law Frameworks</h2>

    <p>Understanding India's cyber laws in isolation is like understanding one piece of a global puzzle. Here's how major international frameworks compare:</p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Country / Framework</th><th>Key Law</th><th>Notable Feature</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>United States</td><td>Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA), 1986</td><td>Criminalises "exceeding authorised access" to computers; very broadly interpreted</td></tr>
        <tr><td>European Union</td><td>GDPR (2018) + NIS Directive</td><td>World's strictest data protection; fines up to 4% of global turnover</td></tr>
        <tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>Computer Misuse Act, 1990</td><td>First law specifically targeting hacking; life imprisonment for serious offences</td></tr>
        <tr><td>China</td><td>Cybersecurity Law, 2017</td><td>Mandatory data localisation; strict content regulation; government access provisions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>India</td><td>IT Act 2000 + DPDP Act 2023</td><td>Comprehensive cybercrime coverage; 65B evidence framework; recent privacy law</td></tr>
        <tr><td>International</td><td>Budapest Convention on Cybercrime, 2001</td><td>First international treaty on cybercrime; India is not a signatory but aligns broadly</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">🌍 India's Position</div>
      <p>India has not signed the Budapest Convention (the primary international cybercrime treaty) primarily over sovereignty concerns about cross-border data access provisions. However, India cooperates through bilateral Mutual Legal Assistance Treaties (MLATs) with over 40 countries for cybercrime investigations. India's DPDP Act 2023 brought its data protection framework closer to GDPR standards.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

